File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Working Context Terms,  Next: Editing Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.1 Your working context
=========================

Buffer
     An Emacs object containing a document or application view.  It is
     not necessarily a file, and switching buffers does not close the
     one you leave.  A window displays a buffer.  See *note Buffers hold
     your work: (emacsvox)Desktop Objects.
Window
     A view of a buffer inside an Emacs frame.  Removing a window does
     not kill its buffer.  Two windows showing the same buffer are views
     of the same text, not independent copies.  See *note Working with
     windows: (emacsvox)Working With Windows.
Frame
     An outer Emacs application window, or the corresponding display in
     a terminal session.  It contains Emacs windows.  Selecting another
     window within a frame differs from switching applications in the
     operating system.  See *note Windows and frames: (emacsvox)Working
     With Windows.
Point
     The current editing position, between characters.  Text is normally
     inserted there.  Short reading often leaves it unchanged; tracked
     reading advances it.  See *note Reading position and interruption:
     (emacsvox)Reading From Point.
Mode
     The commands and behaviour associated with a buffer.  Its major
     mode supplies its principal tools; minor modes add other behaviour.
     See *note Let modes give each buffer its tools: (emacsvox)Object
     Oriented Desktop.
Read-only
     A buffer state that prevents ordinary text edits.  It does not mean
     the buffer has no unsaved changes, and making a buffer writable
     does not grant permission to overwrite a protected file.  Special
     applications may still provide their own operations.  See *note
     Opening a document safely: (emacsvox)Opening A Document.
Narrowing
     Restricting access to part of a buffer without deleting the rest.
     Reading, movement and many editing commands stay within that
     portion; widening removes the restriction.  Ordinary file saving
     still saves the entire buffer.  See *note Reading accessible text:
     (emacsvox)Reading Without Moving Point. and *note Narrowing:
     (emacs)Narrowing. in the GNU Emacs Manual.
Captured source
     The buffer and position retained when a settings workflow starts.
     Contextual actions continue to refer to that source rather than
     whichever menu or editor is currently selected.  A changed or
     closed source may need to be captured again.  See *note Getting
     started with settings menus: (emacsvox)Aural Home.
Scope
     Where a setting or rule applies, such as one buffer, a session, or
     global defaults.  Scope is not the same as persistence: a global
     change need not be saved, and a saved resource need not be active.
     See *note Choose what persists: (emacsvox)Saving And Startup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Editing Terms,  Next: Presentation Resource Terms,  Prev: Working Context Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.2 Selection, copying and editing
===================================

Mark
     A remembered position in a buffer.  Point and mark delimit the
     region, whichever comes first.  The mark can remain after the
     selection is deactivated; it is not copied text.  See *note
     Selecting and checking text: (emacsvox)Selecting Text With Speech.
Region or selection
     The text between point and mark, including any selected spaces and
     newlines.  The character at its end is excluded.  The active region
     is the current selection; some commands can also use an inactive
     mark.  Establish a fresh region before cutting.  See *note
     Selection boundaries and activation: (emacsvox)Selecting Text With
     Speech.
Transient Mark mode
     Emacs's usual active-selection behaviour, used by the editing
     examples.  It is unrelated to the Transient package that supplies
     settings menus.  See *note Using the region through speech:
     (emacsvox)Selecting Text With Speech. and *note The mark and the
     region: (emacs)Mark. in the GNU Emacs Manual.
Copy
     Store a passage in the kill ring while leaving its source text
     unchanged.  The destination becomes an independent text copy when
     you paste it.  See *note Copying between buffers: (emacsvox)Copying
     Text Between Buffers.
Kill or cut
     Remove text and keep it in the kill ring for later insertion.  Some
     deletion commands do not keep that copy.  Killing a buffer is a
     different operation: it closes the buffer rather than copying its
     contents.  See *note Moving text safely: (emacsvox)Moving Text
     Safely.
Yank or paste
     Insert text from the kill ring, or through configured clipboard
     integration, at point.  It changes the destination buffer, not
     automatically its saved file.  See *note Checking a paste:
     (emacsvox)Copying Text Between Buffers.
Kill ring
     Emacs's shared history of copied and cut text.  It can contain more
     than the latest passage, including text placed there by inspection
     commands.  It is not a permanent backup or the same store as the
     system clipboard.  See *note Choosing an earlier copy:
     (emacsvox)Choosing Earlier Copied Text.
Clipboard
     The operating system's text-transfer facility.  Its connection to
     Emacs depends on the display session and configuration; a new
     external copy can affect the next paste.  See *note Testing
     clipboard exchange: (emacsvox)Copying Through The Clipboard.
Undo
     Reverse recorded edits in the selected buffer.  It does not reverse
     a settings save or an action already sent to another application.
     When listening between successive corrections, use the recovery
     guide's ‘undo-only’ procedure.  See *note Undo and spoken
     inspection: (emacsvox)Recovering Text Edits.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Resource Terms,  Next: Speech And Settings Terms,  Prev: Editing Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.3 Voices and presentation resources
======================================

Physical voice
     A voice reported by a speech engine, such as an installed system
     voice or a downloaded model's voice.  Installation alone does not
     prove that it is enabled, active, or audible.  See *note Installed
     voices and engines: (emacsvox)Installed Voices And Engines.
Named voice
     A presentation name such as ‘default’ or ‘bolden’, resolved through
     a voice palette to physical choices and adjustments.  Editing a
     shared named voice can affect several kinds of text that use it.
     See *note Edit a named voice: (emacsvox)Editing Named Voices.
Voice palette
     A collection defining named voices, including their physical
     choices, fallbacks, and adjustments.  Palettes can inherit values;
     editing one is different from changing an engine preference.  See
     *note Voices and routing: (emacsvox)Voices And Routing.
Presentation rule
     A match between application meaning or context and the feedback to
     produce, such as a voice, cue, or silence.  A rule chooses
     presentation; it does not install a physical voice.  See *note
     Manage feedback rules: (emacsvox)Managing Feedback Rules.
Auditory icon or cue
     A short sound conveying an event or state.  A sound pack supplies
     cues, while presentation rules determine when to use them.  Tones
     can convey information without a recorded sound file.  See *note
     Sounds and space: (emacsvox)Sounds And Space.
Presentation profile
     A saved selection of presentation resources and options.  It refers
     to resources rather than copying everything into a full
     configuration backup.  Applying a profile also retains its
     selection for startup; individual palette operations have different
     rules.  See *note Presentation profiles: (emacsvox)Presentation
     Profiles.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech And Settings Terms,  Prev: Presentation Resource Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.4 Changes and speech output
==============================

Preview
     An audition or comparison of a proposed result.  Previewing does
     not by itself save the draft or make it the ordinary live
     configuration.
Retained draft
     An unfinished edit kept in the current Emacs session for return to
     its editor.  Retaining it is not a promise of recovery after Emacs
     exits, and discarding it does not undo a save already completed.
Save
     Record data in the store owned by that editor or setting.  Read the
     action's label: _Save and apply_ and _Save to collection without
     selecting_ intentionally have different effects.
Apply
     Put a chosen configuration into use.  The operation may require
     confirmation, validation, or a worker restart.  Its exact effect
     depends on the resource; it is not a universal synonym for saving.
Startup selection
     The configuration to select when a later session starts.  Selecting
     something for this session does not always select it for the next
     one.  *note Choose what persists: (emacsvox)Saving And Startup.
     compares these states and their owners.
Speech server, engine, and worker
     Emacsvox talks to a speech server; the server uses engines to
     synthesise speech.  The manual uses _worker_ for a running speech
     instance whose configuration and reported voices can differ from
     another instance or from installed files.  See *note Speech
     Backends: (emacsvox)Speech Backends.
Main and notification speech
     Separate output paths for foreground reading and other
     announcements.  An integration decides which path to use for an
     event; their existence is not a guarantee that all background text
     is private.  See *note Speech output and notifications:
     (emacsvox)Aural Speech Output.


